With resolution scaling, a mip level that is clamped to 1 texel along an axis at the guest size is loaded as a whole
scale factor of texels, while that level of the scaled host texture is max((size * scale) >> level, 1), so the copy
goes past the destination subresource and the Direct3D 12 runtime rejects the command list (Close() E_INVALIDARG).
The copy is limited to the destination level, aligned to the host block size as CopyTextureRegion requires for
block-compressed formats. Unscaled textures are not affected.

That clamp only protected the destination; the footprint itself (both the packed mip tail's one shared footprint
and each non-packed level's own) was still sized from the guest mip extent scaled afterward (mip-first), one step
earlier than the box, which is scale-first: (base >> level) * scale can be exactly one row or column short of
(base * scale) >> level, since shifting the already-scaled dimension loses less precision (1280x720 at 4x, mip 6:
(720>>6)*4 = 44 against (720*4)>>6 = 45). Mip 6 is where the packed tail starts for this texture
(GetPackedMipLevel(1280, 720) = 6), so its shared footprint was exactly one row short of the box the copy needed,
and the runtime rejected it outright (Close() E_INVALIDARG, no clamp to fall back on there). For non-packed levels
the box-vs-footprint clamp above hid the same shortfall by silently copying one row or column less than the correct
mip. Mip-first is not simply wrong, though: once a guest mip has collapsed to 1 texel, the load shader still writes
a whole scale factor of host texels there regardless of level, which can itself exceed scale-first (1280x720 at 4x,
mip 10: (720>>10)*4 = 4 against (720*4)>>10 = 2). The footprint now takes the larger of the two at every level, so
it is always at least as large as whichever the shader actually writes, and the packed tail's shared footprint
takes the tightest bound that covers every level it serves.

